@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+PR #51 fixes the ordinary notes-only and tests-only cases. After installing or syncing 0.1.8, a project that gates on `changed --strict` will get exit 1 for an unanswered component. A subsystem advisory alone still exits 0. Two cases can give that project a green gate while a changed component still needs an answer.
+
+### Findings
+
+| ID | Finding and reproduction |
+|---|---|
+| **F1 · Blocking** | **Deleting an old `reread:` line counts as answering the new change.** Start with a component that has a populated `reread:`, change its module, delete the line, regenerate, and run `changed --strict`. I reproduced exit 0 with only the subsystem advisory. Replacing the old line with an empty `- reread:` also exited 0. The comparison accepts any difference, even when the current entry contains no answer for `review` to date. The component should still be printed and strict mode should exit 1. [components_index.py:1213](https://github.com/vasiloglou/dev-tools/blob/8475b62/skills/components/scripts/components_index.py#L1213) |
+| **F2 · Blocking** | **Removing a module can hide the component that lost its capability.** Give `core.sizes` two modules, delete one source file, remove its path from `modules:`, and leave `does` and `verbs` alone. After regeneration, I got `0 to fix, 0 warning(s), 0 to reread` and exit 0. The command checks ownership only in the current catalog. It should also consult ownership at the base and print `core.sizes (only modules edited)` with the deleted path. Its subsystem advisory was lost too. [components_index.py:1209](https://github.com/vasiloglou/dev-tools/blob/8475b62/skills/components/scripts/components_index.py#L1209) |
+| **F3 · Completeness** | **The diagnostic can name an edited entry as untouched.** With a module change, editing the free-text field `owner_team` from `café` to `Δ` printed `only owner_team edited`. The same edit to `audit-owner` or `owner2` printed `entry not edited`. The skill permits “any other key,” but the field matcher recognizes only `[a-z_]+`. Strict mode correctly exited 1 in all three cases. [Component field parser:236](https://github.com/vasiloglou/dev-tools/blob/8475b62/skills/components/scripts/components_index.py#L236) · [Entry format:114](https://github.com/vasiloglou/dev-tools/blob/8475b62/skills/components/SKILL.md#L114) |
+| **F4 · Completeness** | **The shipped command table gives the wrong condition for a subsystem advisory.** It ties the advisory to a member whose dating fields stayed unchanged. I changed a member’s code and its `does`, leaving the subsystem alone. Strict mode printed just the subsystem and exited 0. The table should say that a changed member triggers a check of the **subsystem’s own** dating fields. The site’s `Reread` bullet also omits this advisory case. [Command table:60](https://github.com/vasiloglou/dev-tools/blob/8475b62/skills/components/SKILL.md#L60) · [Site:162](https://github.com/vasiloglou/dev-tools/blob/8475b62/docs/components.html#L162) |
+| **F5 · Inherited compatibility issue** | The blame-header matcher accepts exactly 40 hex digits. Given a valid 64-digit SHA-256 porcelain header, `entry_commits` returned `('', 100, 'does')`. `review` skips that entry because its commit is empty, so a SHA-256 repository can get an empty stale-description list despite having history. I reproduced this with porcelain input in memory. [Blame matcher:1345](https://github.com/vasiloglou/dev-tools/blob/8475b62/skills/components/scripts/components_index.py#L1345) · [Review filter:1413](https://github.com/vasiloglou/dev-tools/blob/8475b62/skills/components/scripts/components_index.py#L1413) |
+
+### What checked out
+
+- **C1 and C9.** Both phase workflows direct the author to answer in the entry and name it in the record or close commit. I found no remaining instruction to answer solely in the record. The example workflow uses `--strict`, and `changed --help` distinguishes component failures from subsystem advice. The review reference already directs a reader to update `reread:` for an accurate entry.
+- **C2.** Both commands use `DATING`. They still answer different questions about time. On this checkout, `changed --base 0b95fbb --strict` exited 0 with `0 to reread`, while `review` reported five module commits after the last dating edit for `sync.copies`. An answer early in a branch can therefore cover later code changes for the strict gate. I also tried a tab and spaces after `reread:`. The catalog parser returned the same value, while real `git blame --contents -` dated the reformatted line as newly edited.
+- **C3–C8.** The ordinary field edits, unchanged old answer, new entry, component/subsystem combinations, and summary counts behaved as claimed, subject to F1–F3. Plain `changed` exited 0 for rereads and 1 for an explicit `fail:`. A retired-caller warning still exited 1 under `--strict`. The new instructional `note:` appeared only for a strict component reread. The existing upstream-selection `note:` remains independent.
+- **C10–C12.** Both supplied SHA-256 hashes matched. The plugin build check and repository index check exited 0. Release status reported 0.1.8 ready and exited 1 as documented. I replayed the added test cases against the historical scripts using in-memory files and base snapshots. The first pairing produced the stated **10 failures**, the second produced **5**, and all **21** final cases succeeded at head. The record follows both code commits, identifies the omitted plan, gates, review round and close, and all nine evidence hashes match `8ae1e84`.
+
+### Verification limits
+
+The review brief requires read-only operation. The full pytest suite, historical pytest commands, fresh install or sync, and `ci_local.sh` create files, so I did not independently verify their recorded end-to-end runs. The downstream reproductions used the product code with in-memory fixtures. The plugin checks and the comparison against `0b95fbb` ran directly on this checkout.
+
+VERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED
